@page "/unsubscribe"
@inject SubscriberStore Subscribers

<PageTitle>One a Day — Unsubscribe</PageTitle>

@* One click: opening the link from an email unsubscribes, with nothing else to press.

   It happens when the page goes live in a browser, not when the page is fetched. Some
   mail filters, mostly corporate ones, open every link in incoming mail to scan it; if the
   plain fetch unsubscribed, those readers would be removed the moment the digest landed,
   without ever seeing it. A scanner downloads the HTML (the prerender, where this does
   nothing) — it doesn't run the page and hold the live connection open, which is the step
   that does the work. Not airtight: a scanner driving a full browser would still count.
   But it stops the common case and costs a real reader nothing.

   Mail clients' own "Unsubscribe" buttons skip this page entirely and use the RFC 8058
   POST endpoint (SubscriptionEndpoints). *@
<div class="oad-static">
    <div class="oad-banner">Unsubscribe</div>

    <div class="oad-box sub-box">
        @if (_outcome == Outcome.Pending)
        {
            <p class="sub-lead">Unsubscribing…</p>
        }
        else if (_outcome == Outcome.Unsubscribed)
        {
            <p class="sub-mark" aria-hidden="true">👋</p>
            <p class="sub-lead">Sorry to see you go — you're now unsubscribed.</p>
            <p class="sub-sub">
                Your address has been deleted from the list, not just switched off, so you
                won't get another email from us. Changed your mind? You can sign up again
                from the bottom of the home page.
            </p>
            <a class="sub-home" href="">Back to the Challenge of the day</a>
        }
        else
        {
            @* Deleting on unsubscribe means a used link and a mangled one look the same
               from here, so this can't promise the emails have stopped — only point at
               the fix if they haven't. *@
            <p class="sub-lead">You're not subscribed.</p>
            <p class="sub-sub">
                This link doesn't match a subscription — most likely you've already
                unsubscribed. If emails are still arriving, use the unsubscribe link in the
                newest one.
            </p>
            <a class="sub-home" href="">Back to the Challenge of the day</a>
        }
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    private enum Outcome { Pending, Unsubscribed, NotFound }

    [SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = "token")]
    public string? Token { get; set; }

    private Outcome _outcome = Outcome.Pending;

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        // Not interactive = the prerender, which is all a link scanner ever sees.
        if (!RendererInfo.IsInteractive)
        {
            return;
        }

        _outcome = Subscribers.Unsubscribe(Token) ? Outcome.Unsubscribed : Outcome.NotFound;
    }
}
